'''Alexander Philippe Albon Ansusinha''' ({{langx|th|อเล็กซานเดอร์ อัลบอน อังศุสิงห์}};{{efn|Ansusinha ({{RTGS|Angsusing}}) is his maternal Thai surname, {{IPA|th|ʔāŋ.sùʔ.sǐŋ|pron}}.}} born 23 March 1996) is a Thai and British [[racing driver]] who competes [[Formula One drivers from Thailand|under the Thai flag]] in [[Formula One]] for [[Williams Racing|Williams]].

Born in [[City of Westminster|Westminster]] and raised in [[Suffolk]], Albon is the son of English racing driver [[Nigel Albon]] and his Thai wife, as well as the nephew of [[Mark Albon]]. After a successful [[karting]] career—culminating in his victories at the [[KF3|junior]] [[Karting World Cup|World Cup]] and [[Karting European Championship|European Championship]] in 2010—Albon graduated to [[junior formulae]]. Following three seasons in the [[Formula Renault Eurocup]] from [[2012 Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0|2012]] to [[2014 Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0|2014]], Albon progressed to the [[FIA Formula 3 European Championship]] in {{FIAF3|2015}}. He moved to the [[GP3 Series]] for [[2016 GP3 Series|2016]], finishing runner-up to [[Charles Leclerc]] in his rookie season with [[ART Grand Prix|ART]]. Graduating to [[FIA Formula 2]] in {{F2|2017}}, Albon finished third in the championship the [[2018 Formula 2 Championship|following season]] with [[DAMS]].

Previously a member of the [[Red Bull Junior Team]] from 2008 to 2012, Albon signed for [[Toro Rosso]] in {{F1|2019}}, making his Formula One debut at the {{F1GP|2019|Australian}}; after 12 races, he was promoted to parent team [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]], replacing [[Pierre Gasly]] to partner [[Max Verstappen]]. In {{F1|2020}}, he became the [[Formula One drivers from Thailand|first Thai driver]] to achieve a [[Podium#In motorsport|podium]] in Formula One at the {{F1GP|2020|Tuscan}}, which he repeated in [[2020 Bahrain Grand Prix|Bahrain]]. Replaced by [[Sergio Pérez]] in {{F1|2021}}, Albon continued as a [[reserve driver]] for Red Bull and the re-branded [[Scuderia AlphaTauri|AlphaTauri]], contesting [[2021 Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters|DTM]] with [[AF Corse|Red Bull AF Corse]] alongside [[Liam Lawson]]. Albon signed for [[Williams Racing|Williams]] in {{F1|2022}} to replace [[George Russell (racing driver)|George Russell]], ending his association with Red Bull at the conclusion of his first season. He remained at Williams through the {{F1|2023}}, {{F1|2024}}, and {{F1|2025}} seasons, finishing eighth—the highest of the [[Glossary of motorsport terms#M|midfield]] drivers—in the latter.

As of the {{Latest F1GP}}, Albon has achieved {{Numbertoword|{{F1stat|ALB|podiums}}}} podium finishes and {{Numbertoword|{{F1stat|ALB|fastestlaps}}}} fastest lap in Formula One. He is contracted to remain at Williams until at least the end of the 2027 season.

== Early life ==
Alexander Philippe Albon Ansusinha was born at the [[Portland Hospital]] in the [[City of Westminster]], London, England, on 23 March 1996.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/information.pl?cite=v66%2BmYUMKGhZkcGbxuYsqA&scan=1|title=Index entry|accessdate=24 October 2025|work=FreeBMD|publisher=ONS}}</ref> His father, [[Nigel Albon]] is a British former racing driver who participated in the [[British Touring Car Championship]] and [[Porsche Carrera Cup]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.driverdb.com/drivers/nigel-albon/|title=Nigel Albon|publisher=Driver Database|date=30 August 2019|access-date=29 August 2019|archive-date=12 August 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190812124418/https://www.driverdb.com/drivers/nigel-albon/|url-status=live}}</ref> His mother, Kankamol "Minky" Albon ({{nee}} Ansusinha), originates from Thailand. His uncle, [[Mark Albon]], is a former racing driver who competed in one round of [[International Formula 3000]].

Growing up in [[Bures, England|Bures]], [[Suffolk]] alongside a younger brother, Luca, and three sisters, Chloe, Zoe and Alicia,<ref name="get-to-know">{{Cite web|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/redbullracing/get-to-know-your-driver-alex-albon|title=Get To Know Your Driver: Alex Albon|access-date=30 July 2020|website=Red Bull|language=en|archive-date=29 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029085933/https://www.redbull.com/int-en/redbullracing/get-to-know-your-driver-alex-albon|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon attended [[Ipswich School]] before leaving to pursue his professional racing career,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sandalls|first=Katy|title=Former Suffolk schoolboy gets top F1 seat|url=https://www.eadt.co.uk/news/ipswich-school-s-alex-albon-moves-to-red-bull-1-6211170|access-date=30 July 2020|website=East Anglian Daily Times|date=12 August 2019|language=en|archive-date=13 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200813134522/https://www.eadt.co.uk/news/ipswich-school-s-alex-albon-moves-to-red-bull-1-6211170|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=5 things you didn't know about 2019 Scuderia Toro Rosso driver Alexander Albon|url=https://www.redbull.com/au-en/alexander-albon-profile|access-date=30 July 2020|website=Red Bull|date=28 November 2018 |language=en|archive-date=29 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029085939/https://www.redbull.com/au-en/alexander-albon-profile|url-status=live}}</ref> citing [[Michael Schumacher]] and [[Valentino Rossi]] as being inspirational figures when he was younger.<ref name="get-to-know"/>

== Junior racing career ==
=== Karting (2005–2011) ===
Albon started competitively racing karts in 2005 at the age of eight, competing locally and winning his local [[Hoddesdon]] Championship at [[Rye House Kart Circuit]]. In 2006, Albon started racing in the cadet class, finishing first at the Kartmasters British Grand Prix and participating in the [[Super 1 National Kart Championships|Super 1 National Honda Cadet Championship]] finishing first in 2006 and second in 2007. In 2008, he was signed to the [[Red Bull Junior Team]]<ref>{{cite web |last1=Maxifahrer |first1=Alex |title=Who is Alexander Albon? – Toro Rosso driver profile |url=https://www.redbull.com/car-en/alexander-albon-profile |website=Redbull.com |access-date=26 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251226173808/https://www.redbull.com/car-en/alexander-albon-profile |url-status=live |archive-date=26 December 2025 |date=27 November 2018}}</ref> and moved up to the [[KF3]] class, where he stayed until 2010. During this time, Albon won the Kartmasters British Grand Prix, [[Formula Kart Stars|Formula Kart Stars Championship]], KF Winter Series, Super 1 National KF3 Championship, [[Karting World Championship|CIK-FIA World Cup]], and [[CIK-FIA Karting European Championship|CIK-FIA European Championship]]. In 2011, Albon graduated to [[KF1]] placing second in the [[WSK Euro Series]] and second at the [[Karting World Championship|CIK-FIA World Championship]].

=== Formula Renault 2.0 (2012–2014) ===
==== 2012: Junior formulae debut ====
From karting, Albon graduated to the [[Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0|Formula Renault 2.0 Eurocup series]] where he drove for [[EPIC Racing]] in 2012 alongside [[Kevin Giovesi]], [[Konstantin Tereshchenko|Konstantin Tereschenko]], [[Kevin Jörg]], Dennis Wusthoff and Christof von Grunigen and finished 38th out of 49 in the championship after having a tough year and being unable to score points. Albon was dropped by Red Bull at the end of 2012.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/introducing-alex-albon|title=Introducing Alex Albon...|work=Red Bull|access-date=22 January 2018|language=en|archive-date=27 November 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181127193845/https://www.redbull.com/int-en/introducing-alex-albon|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-red-bull-had-no-reason-to-keep-me-in-junior-programme-in-2012.7Dvcbx44glK2TIS6qKXkK5|title=Albon: Red Bull 'had no reason' to keep me in junior programme in 2012|date=27 August 2019|access-date=20 October 2024|website=Formula 1}}</ref>

==== 2013: Maiden pole position ====
In 2013, Albon was signed to [[Alpine Academy|Lotus F1 Academy]]<ref>{{cite web |title=Lotus select seven junior team drivers |url=http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2013/2/14271.html |publisher=Formula1.com |access-date=9 September 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130218092235/http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2013/2/14271.html |archive-date=18 February 2013 |date=15 February 2013}}</ref> and joined [[Kurt Mollekens|KTR]] to race in the [[Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0]] season alongside [[Yu Kanamaru]] and Ignazio D'Agosto, finishing 16th out of 36 in the championship. Albon managed to secure one fastest lap and one pole position in the 2013 season, both of them coming at the [[Red Bull Ring]] in [[Austria]]. He finished the 2013 season with 22 points.

==== 2014: Third in the Eurocup ====
In 2014, Albon raced alongside [[Gregor Ramsay]], [[Jules Gounon]] and Callan O'Keefe and enjoyed a much more successful year. He was once again unable to find a win at any of the 14 races but managed to get one pole position at the [[Nürburgring]] and finished third in the drivers' championship with 117 points.

=== Formula Three (2015) ===
[[File:Alexander Albon, Formel 3 2015.JPG|thumb|Albon at Spa in 2015, European Formula 3 Championship]]
In 2015, Albon switched to [[FIA European Formula Three Championship|FIA European Formula 3]], racing at [[Signature Team|Signature]] with teammate [[Dorian Boccolacci]]. He finished seventh overall, with two pole positions (scored at the [[Norisring]]), 5 podiums (including four rookie wins), and 187 points overall.

=== GP3 Series (2016) ===
In December 2015, Albon partook in post-season testing with [[ART Grand Prix]]. In 2016, Albon raced for ART in the [[2016 GP3 Series season|GP3]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.motorsport.com/gp3/news/de-vries-joins-2016-gp3-field-with-art-675332/|title=De Vries joins 2016 GP3 field with ART|date=24 February 2016|access-date=24 February 2016|quote="We have Charles Leclerc, Alexander Albon [neither confirmed yet], Jake Hughes, Jack Aitken and Kevin Jorg lining up on the grid and Antonio Fuoco is staying for another season. All these guys are capable of winning races"|archive-date=30 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180630105323/https://www.motorsport.com/gp3/news/de-vries-joins-2016-gp3-field-with-art-675332/|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon claimed four wins and finished as runner-up in the championship to teammate [[Charles Leclerc (racing driver)|Charles Leclerc]].

=== FIA Formula 2 (2017–2018) ===
==== 2017: Rookie season and maiden podiums ====
In 2017, Albon graduated to the [[FIA Formula 2 Championship]], with [[ART Grand Prix|ART]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.motorsport.com/gp2/news/gp3-runner-up-albon-confirmed-for-gp2-2017-promotion-875840/|title=GP3 runner-up Albon confirmed for GP2 2017 promotion|date=22 February 2017|access-date=22 February 2017|archive-date=22 February 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170222201355/https://www.motorsport.com/gp2/news/gp3-runner-up-albon-confirmed-for-gp2-2017-promotion-875840/|url-status=live}}</ref> His teammate for the season would be [[Nobuharu Matsushita]], who at the time was also signed as a [[Driver development program|development driver]] for [[McLaren]]. He made his debut in [[2017 Sakhir Formula 2 round|Bahrain]], where he started in ninth place on the starting grid for the feature race and finished sixth. For the sprint race, Albon qualified third on the grid, behind [[Luca Ghiotto]] and his teammate, Matsushita. However, mechanical problems forced Matsushita to start from the pitlane promoting Albon to second. Albon struggled for grip for the majority of the race and finished in seventh position.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.autosport.com/f2/news/129011/clever-strategy-hands-leclerc-f2-sprint-win|title=Bahrain Formula 2: Ferrari junior Leclerc beats Ghiotto and Rowland|last=Kalinauckas|first=Alex|work=Autosport.com|access-date=22 January 2018|language=en|archive-date=13 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171213010246/https://www.autosport.com/f2/news/129011/clever-strategy-hands-leclerc-f2-sprint-win|url-status=live}}</ref>

At the [[2017 Barcelona Formula 2 round|Spanish round]], Albon placed fifth on the provisional starting grid for the feature race. He made a strong start, moving to third behind [[Charles Leclerc]] and Ghiotto. Leclerc began to pull away from Ghiotto, who began to fall into the clutches of Albon, who subsequently made a move into turn one and took second place from Ghiotto. Leclerc pitted on lap seven, leaving Albon with the lead of the race. After three-lap safety car period beginning on on lap 10, [[Oliver Rowland]] eventually pressured Albon into a mistake to take the lead of the race, although both still had an impending pitstop to make. With the fresh rubber, Albon and Rowland were staging a comeback with both drivers challenging for the podium toward the latter stages of the race. Albon finished the race in fifth position.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-05-13 |title=Leclerc scorches to feature victory |url=http://www.fiaformula2.com/News-Room/News/2017/05_May/Leclerc-scorches-to-feature-victory-/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170515084224/http://www.fiaformula2.com/News-Room/News/2017/05_May/Leclerc-scorches-to-feature-victory-/ |archive-date=2017-05-15 |access-date=2026-05-28 |website=[[FIA Formula 2]]}}</ref> In the sprint race, Albon started fourth on the grid and enjoyed a well-fought battle with Leclerc for the majority of the race and after battling for several laps, Leclerc finally passed Albon for fifth place. Later in the race however, Albon dropped back, finishing the race in eighth position.

At the [[2017 Monte Carlo Formula 2 round|Monaco round]], Albon qualified second on the grid with a time of 1:19.321 seconds. In qualifying, the grid was separated into two Groups due to safety concerns over the short and tight nature of the circuit. Albon was part of the 'Group B' qualifying and managed to gain the fastest time in that group, only qualifying 12 hundredths of a second behind Leclerc who qualified in Group A. After an aborted start due to [[Antonio Fuoco]] and [[Sean Gelael]]'s engines [[stall (engine)|stalling]] on the grid, Leclerc led into the first corner, followed by Albon. A concertina effect occurred at the Grand Hotel Hairpin as Canamasas was spun, causing Gelael to lose his front wing and bringing out a local yellow. Later in the race, Albon found himself stuck behind the slower moving [[Norman Nato]] and [[Jordan King]], which eventually caused him to lose places, finishing the race in a disappointing fourth position. In the Sprint Race, Albon started fifth on the grid, and after a very tight race, he dropped back to finish in sixth position. Albon missed the [[2017 Baku Formula 2 round|Baku round]] of the Championship due to injury. Albon had sustained a broken collarbone whilst out on a mountain biking training ride, and was unable to compete due to the over-the-shoulder seat belts used in [[Formula 2]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.autosport.com/f2/news/130275/albon-explains-injury-behind-baku-absence|title=ART F2 driver Alexander Albon explains injury behind Baku absence|last=Kalinauckas|first=Alex|work=Autosport.com|access-date=29 January 2018|language=en|archive-date=30 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180130013819/https://www.autosport.com/f2/news/130275/albon-explains-injury-behind-baku-absence|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.motorsport.com/fia-f2/news/albon-explains-extent-of-baku-missing-injuries-920614/|title=Albon explains extent of cycling crash injuries|work=Motorsport.com|access-date=29 January 2018|language=en|archive-date=29 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180129195234/https://www.motorsport.com/fia-f2/news/albon-explains-extent-of-baku-missing-injuries-920614/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Albon was back in action for the fifth round of the championship, stating that his initial feeling on returning to action after breaking his collarbone was "a lot better" than he expected. He confirmed that the bone was still "clearly broken" following an x-ray on the Tuesday before the race weekend, and explained that the main issue he is having in the car is a "numb feeling" from the scar he received during successful surgery after the crash.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://classic.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/130596|title=F2 news: Injured Formula 2 racer Albon says return better than expected|last=Kalinauckas|first=Alex|website=AUTOSPORT.com|date=7 July 2017 |access-date=29 January 2018|archive-date=30 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180130013835/http://classic.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/130596|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon finished the practice session in eighth, which showed that despite the injury, the chance for his first podium in Formula 2 was a possibility. Albon qualified in fourth for the Feature Race, however, he was later promoted to third on the provisional starting grid after [[Sérgio Sette Câmara]] was disqualified after the qualifying session after failing to provide the required 1 litre fuel sample.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.autosport.com/f2/news/130604/sette-camara-loses-austria-f2-front-row-start|title=Sergio Sette Camara loses Red Bull Ring Formula 2 front row start|last=Kalinauckas|first=Alex|work=Autosport.com|access-date=29 January 2018|language=en|archive-date=30 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180130013754/https://www.autosport.com/f2/news/130604/sette-camara-loses-austria-f2-front-row-start|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon finished the Feature Race in fifth position, after losing places to Oliver Rowland and Nicholas Latifi (both racing for [[DAMS]]) whose car proved to have a lot of pace. For the Sprint Race, Albon started the race fourth on the grid and managed to move up the grid to clinch his first podium in Formula 2, finishing behind [[Artem Markelov]]. He would later score another podium at the sprint race at the season finale in [[2017 Yas Island Formula 2 round|Abu Dhabi]], finishing in second after being overtaken by Leclerc on the final lap. He finished tenth in the drivers' championship in his first F2 season, scoring 86 points.

==== 2018: Third to Russell and Norris ====
[[File:FIA F2 Austria 2018 Nr. 05 Albon (2).jpg|thumb|Albon at [[Red Bull Ring]] in 2018|left]] [[File:2018 FIA Formula 2 Championship, Silverstone Circuit (43693586962).jpg|thumb|Albon on the podium after winning the [[2018 Silverstone Formula 2 round]]]]In April 2018, [[DAMS]] announced that they signed Albon for the [[2018 FIA Formula 2 Championship|2018 season]] to partner [[Nicholas Latifi]]. While initially only confirmed for the opening round, he was later confirmed as a full-time driver for the team the following month. He started the season with fourth place in the feature race in [[2018 Sakhir Formula 2 round|Bahrain]] before finishing thirteenth in the sprint race.

For the next round in Baku, Albon started from pole for the feature race and followed it up with his first win in F2, while in the sprint race he finished thirteenth again.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Benyon |first=Jack |date=2018-04-28 |title=F2 Baku: Alexander Albon scores maiden win in frantic feature race |url=https://www.autosport.com/formula2/news/f2-baku-alexander-albon-scores-maiden-win-in-frantic-feature-race-5318563/5318563/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240302102117/https://www.autosport.com/formula2/news/f2-baku-alexander-albon-scores-maiden-win-in-frantic-feature-race-5318563/5318563/ |archive-date=2 March 2024 |access-date=2025-12-11 |work=Autosport |language=en |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Formula 2 2018 Result for Round 2: Azerbaijan, Baku 27-29 April 2018 |url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Results?raceid=984 |access-date=2025-12-11 |website=FIA Formula 2 |language=en}}</ref>

At the next two rounds in [[2018 Barcelona Formula 2 round|Barcelona]] and [[2018 Monte Carlo Formula 2 round|Monaco]], Albon took two more pole positions but finished fifth in the feature race in Spain after getting away slowly while in the sprint he finished second behind [[Jack Aitken]]. In Monaco, however, it was a weekend to forget for the Thai driver, as in the feature race, he collided with [[Nyck de Vries]] as he was entering the pitlane, spinning him around in the pitlane entrance, while in the sprint race he collided with [[Campos Racing|Campos]]' [[Roy Nissany]] approaching the Nouvelle Chicane.

Another retirement would follow in the feature race at [[2018 Le Castellet Formula 2 round|Le Castellet]] after Albon suffered an engine failure. In the sprint race, he finished seventh, one place ahead of Latifi. After finishing fifth in both races at the Red Bull Ring, Albon won the feature race at [[2018 Silverstone Formula 2 round|Silverstone]], before collecting two more wins at the sprint race at [[2018 Budapest Formula 2 round|the Hungaroring]], and the feature race at [[2018 Sochi Formula 2 round|Sochi]]. A stall on the grid in the feature race at Abu Dhabi ended his title chances; he finished fourteenth in the feature race and eighth in the sprint race, leaving him third in the drivers' championship behind fellow future F1 drivers [[George Russell (racing driver)|George Russell]] and [[Lando Norris]].

== Formula One career ==
=== Toro Rosso (2019) ===
[[File:FIA F1 Austria 2019 Nr. 23 Albon 2.jpg|thumb|left|Albon driving for [[Scuderia Toro Rosso|Toro Rosso]] at the [[2019 Austrian Grand Prix]]]]
On 26 November 2018, [[DAMS|Nissan e.dams]] terminated their [[Formula E]] contract with Albon after rumours he was to sign for [[Scuderia Toro Rosso]] in Formula One. On the same day, Toro Rosso announced Albon would join the team for {{F1|2019}} alongside [[Daniil Kvyat]] and thus Albon's relationship with Red Bull Racing, which had ended seven years prior, was restored. He is the second [[Formula One drivers from Thailand|Thai driver]] to compete in Formula One<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.foxsports.co.th/auto-racing/fia-formula-one-world-cship/72371/%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%882-%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2/|title=คนไทยคนที่2! อัลบอนเตรียมซิ่งF1ฤดูกาลหน้า|language=th|date=18 October 2018|access-date=18 August 2019|archive-date=18 August 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190818081821/https://www.foxsports.co.th/auto-racing/fia-formula-one-world-cship/72371/%e0%b8%84%e0%b8%99%e0%b9%84%e0%b8%97%e0%b8%a2%e0%b8%84%e0%b8%99%e0%b8%97%e0%b8%b5%e0%b9%882-%e0%b8%ad%e0%b8%b1%e0%b8%a5%e0%b8%9a%e0%b8%ad%e0%b8%99%e0%b9%80%e0%b8%95%e0%b8%a3%e0%b8%b5%e0%b8%a2/|url-status=dead}}</ref> and the first since [[Birabongse Bhanudej|Prince Birabongse Bhanudej]] competed in {{F1|1954}}.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-to-partner-kvyat-at-toro-rosso-in-2019.2HXzDL0HW8oEUeIqIOsYkq.html|title=Albon to partner Kvyat at Toro Rosso in 2019|date=26 November 2018|website=Formula 1|access-date=11 March 2019|language=en|archive-date=1 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211101135306/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-to-partner-kvyat-at-toro-rosso-in-2019.2HXzDL0HW8oEUeIqIOsYkq.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Albon qualified thirteenth and finished fourteenth in his debut race, the {{F1 GP|2019|Australian}}. He scored his first points at the following race, the {{F1 GP|2019|Bahrain}}, finishing ninth. A heavy crash in practice for the {{F1 GP|2019|Chinese}} forced him to miss qualifying and start the race from the pit lane. He recovered in the race to finish tenth and win the ''Driver Of The Day'' award.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.driver-of-the-day-albon-dedicates-china-point-to-mechanics-after-fp3-smash.F9kstc3uWHBMDC1p1foMB.html |title=Driver Of The Day: Albon dedicates China point to mechanics after FP3 smash |access-date=14 April 2019 |publisher=[[Formula One]] |archive-date=14 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190414101620/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.driver-of-the-day-albon-dedicates-china-point-to-mechanics-after-fp3-smash.F9kstc3uWHBMDC1p1foMB.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He reached the third qualifying session (Q3) for the first time at the {{F1 GP|2019|Monaco}} and finished the race eighth. Damage caused by contact with [[Antonio Giovinazzi]] on the first lap of the {{F1 GP|2019|Canadian}} later led to Albon's first race retirement.

Albon's best qualifying result with Toro Rosso came at the {{F1 GP|2019|British}} with ninth place, although he failed to score points in the race. Albon started the {{F1 GP|2019|German}} in sixteenth place. He and Toro Rosso took advantage of changing weather conditions to run as high as fourth and eventually finish sixth, albeit behind teammate Kvyat who claimed the team's first podium finish in over ten years. At this stage of the season, Albon had scored 16 points to Kvyat's 27.

=== Red Bull (2019–2020) ===
==== 2019: Mid-season promotion ====
[[File:Alexander Albon at 2019 Italian Grand Prix (qualifying).jpg|alt=|thumb|Albon driving for [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] at the [[2019 Italian Grand Prix]]]]
After the {{F1 GP|2019|Hungarian}}, [[Red Bull Racing]] announced that Albon would be replacing [[Pierre Gasly]] and partnering [[Max Verstappen]] at the team from the {{F1 GP|2019|Belgian}} onwards,<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://redbullracing.redbull.com/article/alex-joins-team|title=Alex Joins the Team|website=redbullracing.redbull.com|language=en|access-date=12 August 2019|archive-date=12 August 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190812104837/https://redbullracing.redbull.com/article/alex-joins-team|url-status=live}}</ref> with Gasly returning to Toro Rosso. On the mid-season change, Red Bull stated: "The team will use the next nine races to evaluate Alex's performance in order to make an informed decision as to who will drive alongside Max in 2020."<ref>{{Cite web|last=Harbert|first=Joe|url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/news/f1/red-bull-drops-pierre-gasly-alexander-albon|title=Red Bull drops Pierre Gasly for Alexander Albon|date=12 August 2019|website=Motor Sport Magazine|language=en|access-date=12 August 2019|archive-date=12 August 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190812120931/https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/news/f1/red-bull-drops-pierre-gasly-alexander-albon|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/49317809|title=Red Bull: Alexander Albon to replace Pierre Gasly|date=12 August 2019|access-date=12 August 2019|archive-date=30 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191130141608/https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/49317809|url-status=live}}</ref>

At the Belgian Grand Prix, Albon was forced to start from seventeenth place due to a power unit change. He recovered to finish fifth in the race after passing [[Sergio Pérez]] on the final lap. After sixth-place finishes at the [[2019 Italian Grand Prix|Italian]] and [[2019 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]] Grands Prix, Albon finished fifth at the {{F1 GP|2019|Russian}} having crashed in qualifying and started from the pit lane. Albon and Verstappen set identical lap times in qualifying at the {{F1 GP|2019|Japanese}} and Albon finished a career-best fourth in the race. He finished fifth at both the [[2019 Mexican Grand Prix|Mexican]] and [[2019 United States Grand Prix|United States]] Grands Prix, despite taking damage on the opening lap and making three pit stops at the latter. He was in second place on the penultimate lap of the {{F1 GP|2019|Brazilian}}, but was hit by [[Lewis Hamilton]] during an overtaking attempt, dropping Albon to fourteenth place at the finish. He finished sixth at the [[2019 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi Grand Prix]] to close out the season.

Albon ended his debut season eighth in the World Drivers' Championship with 92 points. He scored 76 points during his nine races at Red Bull, compared to 97 for Verstappen over the same period. Albon received the ''Rookie of the Year'' award at the [[FIA Prize Giving Ceremony]].

==== 2020: Maiden podiums ====
[[File:Alexander Albon-Red Bull RB16 (4).jpg|thumb|Albon driving for Red Bull in [[Circuit de Barcelona-Catalunya|Barcelona]] in 2020 during pre-season testing|left]]
Albon continued racing for Red Bull alongside Verstappen in {{F1|2020}}.<ref name="rbr 2020">{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-to-remain-alongside-verstappen-at-red-bull-for-2020.1NXdYcThqNQuafedKhzCqH.html|title=Alex Albon to remain alongside Max Verstappen at Red Bull for 2020|date=12 November 2019|website=Formula 1|language=en|access-date=12 November 2019|archive-date=12 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191112101207/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-to-remain-alongside-verstappen-at-red-bull-for-2020.1NXdYcThqNQuafedKhzCqH.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In the closing stages of the season-opening {{F1 GP|2020|Austrian}}, Albon was in third place at the [[safety car]] restart on new [[Formula One tyres|soft-compound tyres]], behind the leading [[Mercedes in F1|Mercedes]] cars on older hard tyres. Albon attempted to overtake Lewis Hamilton but the two made contact, sending Albon into the gravel. Albon later retired with an electrical failure which engine supplier [[Honda in F1|Honda]] blamed on the collision.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/150362/albon-hamilton-clash-denied-me-potential-race-win|title=Albon: Hamilton clash denied me potential F1 race win at Austrian GP|website=autosport.com|first=Luke|last=Smith|date=5 July 2020|access-date=6 July 2020|archive-date=5 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200705174616/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/150362/albon-hamilton-clash-denied-me-potential-race-win|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=10 July 2020|title=Albon's DNF likely caused by Hamilton clash, not Honda fault|url=https://the-race.com/formula-1/albons-dnf-likely-caused-by-hamilton-clash-not-honda-fault/|access-date=21 July 2020|website=The Race|language=en-GB|archive-date=21 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200721123813/https://the-race.com/formula-1/albons-dnf-likely-caused-by-hamilton-clash-not-honda-fault/|url-status=live}}</ref> He came under pressure from [[Racing Point F1 Team|Racing Point]]'s Sergio Pérez in the final laps of the {{F1 GP|2020|Styrian}} but maintained fourth place after the drivers made contact, damaging Pérez's front wing.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2020/races/1046/austria/race-result.html|title=Formula 1 Pirelli Grosser Preis der Steiermark 2020 – Race Result|website=Formula 1|date=12 July 2020|access-date=12 July 2020|archive-date=18 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201118093025/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2020/races/1046/austria/race-result.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Red Bull commented that they were unsure why Albon lacked pace in the race.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/red-bull-albon-slow-pace-styria/4833470/?ic_source=home-page-widget&ic_medium=widget&ic_campaign=widget-22|title=F1 news: Red Bull baffled by Albon's Styrian GP struggles|website=Motorsport|date=14 July 2020 |access-date=14 July 2020|archive-date=16 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200716234832/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/red-bull-albon-slow-pace-styria/4833470/?ic_source=home-page-widget&ic_medium=widget&ic_campaign=widget-22|url-status=live}}</ref> He started thirteenth and recovered to fifth at the {{F1 GP|2020|Hungarian}}.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Albon's fifth place confirmed after Red Bull 'grid drying' investigation|url=https://www.racefans.net/2020/07/19/albon-keeps-fifth-place/|website=racefans.net|date=19 July 2020|access-date=3 August 2020|archive-date=15 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201115085349/https://www.racefans.net/2020/07/19/albon-keeps-fifth-place/|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Alexander Albon 2020 Tuscan Grand Prix - race day.jpg|thumb|Albon racing at the [[2020 Tuscan Grand Prix]], where he achieved his maiden podium]]
Albon crashed heavily in practice for the {{F1 GP|2020|British}} and went on to qualify twelfth. He received a penalty in the race for causing a collision with [[Kevin Magnussen]] and dropped to the back of the field before ultimately finishing eighth. He qualified fifth for the {{F1 GP|2020|Belgian}} but finished sixth after being passed by [[Renault in Formula One|Renault]]'s [[Esteban Ocon]] on the final lap.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The Winners and Losers of the 2020 Belgian Grand Prix|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.the-winners-and-losers-of-the-2020-belgian-grand-prix.3yZcRKypzGgWiBtesRDLbR.html|access-date=4 September 2020|website=Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=4 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200904121059/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.the-winners-and-losers-of-the-2020-belgian-grand-prix.3yZcRKypzGgWiBtesRDLbR.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He was fifteenth at the {{F1 GP|2020|Italian}} having taken collision damage and a time penalty on the opening lap.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Italian Grand Prix 2020 race report and highlights: Gasly beats Sainz to maiden win in Monza thriller, as Hamilton recovers to P7 after penalty|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.gasly-takes-maiden-win-in-monza-thriller-from-sainz-as-penalty-leaves.2wjQyiSgOy8ggwcJlWHhsi.html|access-date=6 September 2020|website=Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=6 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200906230949/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.gasly-takes-maiden-win-in-monza-thriller-from-sainz-as-penalty-leaves.2wjQyiSgOy8ggwcJlWHhsi.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon took his maiden Formula One podium at the {{F1 GP|2020|Tuscan}} by overtaking [[Daniel Ricciardo]] in the closing laps, the first podium for a [[Formula One drivers from Thailand|Thai Formula One driver]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=13 September 2020|title=Tuscan GP Facts & Stats: Hamilton just one win shy of Schumacher record|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.tuscan-gp-facts-and-stats-hamilton-just-one-win-shy-of-schumacher-record.7e7BI7Kdlrl6zLnPQZOaad.html|access-date=14 September 2020|archive-date=14 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200914130555/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.tuscan-gp-facts-and-stats-hamilton-just-one-win-shy-of-schumacher-record.7e7BI7Kdlrl6zLnPQZOaad.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Albon claimed only a single point over the next four races; he finished tenth at the {{F1 GP|2020|Russian}}, collided with former teammate Daniil Kvyat and later retired with a damaged radiator at the {{F1 GP|2020|Eifel}},<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.there-was-more-to-come-from-albon-before-pierced-radiator-ended-his-eifel-gp.1xOYlgHocw5BIVVLnC36tC.html|title='There was more to come from Albon' before pierced radiator ended his Eifel GP, says Horner|website=Formula 1|date=12 October 2020|access-date=19 October 2020|archive-date=15 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201015063007/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.there-was-more-to-come-from-albon-before-pierced-radiator-ended-his-eifel-gp.1xOYlgHocw5BIVVLnC36tC.html|url-status=live}}</ref> was lapped by Verstappen and finished twelfth at the {{F1 GP|2020|Portuguese}} and dropped to fifteenth at the {{F1 GP|2020|Emilia Romagna}} due to a spin with five laps remaining. He led a race for the first time at the {{F1 GP|2020|Turkish}} on his way to seventh place, and took his second podium finish at the {{F1 GP|2020|Bahrain}} after Sergio Pérez retired from third place due to an engine failure, making him the first Asian driver to score more than one podium finish.<ref name="Autosport race report">{{Cite news|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/153836/hamilton-wins-bahrain-gp-following-grosjean-crash|title=F1 Bahrain GP: Hamilton wins following Grosjean's fireball crash|last=Kalinauckas|first=Alex|work=[[Autosport]]|date=29 November 2020|access-date=29 November 2020|url-access=limited|language=en|archive-date=29 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201129173253/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/153836/hamilton-wins-bahrain-gp-following-grosjean-crash|url-status=live}}</ref> He finished sixth at the [[2020 Sakhir Grand Prix|Sakhir Grand Prix]], having started twelfth, and finished fourth at the [[2020 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi Grand Prix]], pressuring [[Lewis Hamilton]] in the closing stages of the race.

Albon finished the season seventh in the World Drivers' Championship, scoring 105 points to Verstappen's 214.

=== Red Bull / AlphaTauri reserve driver (2021) ===
Albon was demoted to the role of test and reserve driver with Red Bull for {{F1|2021}}, his race seat being taken by Sergio Pérez.<ref>{{Cite web|date=18 December 2020|title=Perez to partner Verstappen at Red Bull in 2021, as Albon becomes reserve driver|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-perez-to-partner-verstappen-at-red-bull-in-2021-as-albon-becomes.21qHfmHAyfzAjVHT3PfVBd.html|access-date=18 December 2020|website=Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=18 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201218140613/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-perez-to-partner-verstappen-at-red-bull-in-2021-as-albon-becomes.21qHfmHAyfzAjVHT3PfVBd.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Following his demotion, Albon remarked that "it hurts" but added that he hoped to return to a race seat for {{F1|2022}}.<ref>{{cite web|title='It hurts, but I'm not giving up' - Albon resolute as he breaks silence on losing Red Bull race seat|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.it-hurts-but-im-not-giving-up-albon-breaks-silence-on-losing-red-bull-seat.7av3V5do8Ax6loCh3tVKmW.html|access-date=21 December 2020|website=Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=20 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201220150749/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.it-hurts-but-im-not-giving-up-albon-breaks-silence-on-losing-red-bull-seat.7av3V5do8Ax6loCh3tVKmW.html|url-status=live}}</ref> After finishing his [[2021 Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters|2021 DTM]] campaign, he took on a coaching role for [[Scuderia AlphaTauri|AlphaTauri]] driver [[Yuki Tsunoda]] starting from the [[2021 Turkish Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=6 November 2021|title=Albon takes on coaching role for Tsunoda|url=https://www.grandprix.com/news/albon-takes-on-coaching-role-for-tsunoda.html|access-date=5 February 2022|website=Grandprix.com|archive-date=5 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220205031229/https://www.grandprix.com//news/albon-takes-on-coaching-role-for-tsunoda.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Mitchell|first=Scott|date=5 November 2021|title=Albon giving Tsunoda 'a lot of knowledge' in F1 support role|url=https://the-race.com/formula-1/albon-giving-tsunoda-a-lot-of-knowledge-in-f1-support-role/|access-date=5 February 2022|website=The Race|language=en-GB|archive-date=5 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220205030729/https://the-race.com/formula-1/albon-giving-tsunoda-a-lot-of-knowledge-in-f1-support-role/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Valantine |first=Henry |date=9 October 2021 |title=Alex Albon coaching Yuki Tsunoda through the Turkish Grand Prix weekend |url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/alex-albon-coaching-yuki-tsunoda |access-date=29 August 2025 |website=PlanetF1 |language=en}}</ref>

=== Williams (2022–present) ===
==== 2022: Return to new regulations ====
[[File:Alex Albon 2022.jpg|thumb|left|Alex Albon at the {{F1GP||2022 Emilia Romagna}}]]
Albon returned to a Formula One race seat in {{F1|2022}} with [[Williams Grand Prix Engineering|Williams]], replacing [[George Russell (racing driver)|George Russell]] and partnering former Formula 2 teammate [[Nicholas Latifi]].<ref name="williams">{{Cite news|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/news/2021/drivers/williams-racing-confirms-latifi-albon-for-2022|title=Williams Racing confirms Latifi & Albon as 2022 driver line up|work=Williams F1|access-date=8 September 2021|archive-date=8 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210908120814/https://www.williamsf1.com/news/2021/drivers/williams-racing-confirms-latifi-albon-for-2022|url-status=live}}</ref> Red Bull team principal [[Christian Horner]] revealed that Albon retained "a link to Red Bull" and that the team had an option to recall him for {{F1|2023}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.red-bull-retain-option-on-williams-bound-albon-for-2023-reveals-horner.77XgULYCJyeK24jJ43ic6C.html|title=Red Bull retain option on Williams-bound Albon for 2023, reveals Horner|website=Formula 1|date=17 September 2021|accessdate=5 January 2022|archive-date=5 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220105210547/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.red-bull-retain-option-on-williams-bound-albon-for-2023-reveals-horner.77XgULYCJyeK24jJ43ic6C.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

In his first race for Williams, the {{F1 GP|2022|Bahrain}}, Albon out-qualified Latifi and finished the race thirteenth.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Report: Alex P13 and Nicky P16 at season opener|date=21 March 2022|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/ac42e1fa-9dbd-4b2f-b17e-210fe81e34d7/bahrain-grand-prix-2022-race-report|access-date=10 September 2022|website=[[Williams Racing]]|archive-date=26 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220826120951/https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/ac42e1fa-9dbd-4b2f-b17e-210fe81e34d7/bahrain-grand-prix-2022-race-report|url-status=live}}</ref> He was in twelfth place in the final laps of the {{F1 GP|2022|Saudi Arabian}}, but failed to finish after a collision with [[Lance Stroll]] for which Albon was penalised.<ref>{{Cite web|title='I don't regret the move' says Albon – despite grid penalty for contact with Stroll|date=28 March 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.i-dont-regret-the-move-says-albon-despite-grid-penalty-for-contact-with.3ijkgKseEjCjnPR6rrEPpu.html|access-date=10 September 2022|website=Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=20 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220420124813/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.i-dont-regret-the-move-says-albon-despite-grid-penalty-for-contact-with.3ijkgKseEjCjnPR6rrEPpu.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He scored his first point for Williams at the {{F1 GP|2022|Australian}} by finishing tenth; he started the race from last place and made his mandatory pit stop with one lap remaining.<ref>{{Cite web|title=F1 Australian GP: Leclerc scores dominant win as Verstappen retires|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/f1-australian-gp-leclerc-scores-dominant-win-as-verstappen-retires/9768584/|access-date=10 April 2022|date=10 April 2022|website=autosport.com|language=en|archive-date=10 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220410064943/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/f1-australian-gp-leclerc-scores-dominant-win-as-verstappen-retires/9768584/|url-status=live}}</ref> He again started last at the {{F1 GP|2022|Emilia Romagna}} due to a brake fire in qualifying,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Report: "We did not rise to the occasion" on Quali day in Imola|date=23 April 2022|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/d017f4ae-c6fa-410b-b236-8cddee32f0bc/qualifying-report-2022-emilia-romagna-grand-prix|access-date=10 September 2022|website=[[Williams Racing]]|archive-date=10 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910125029/https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/d017f4ae-c6fa-410b-b236-8cddee32f0bc/qualifying-report-2022-emilia-romagna-grand-prix|url-status=live}}</ref> but recovered to finish the race eleventh.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Albon says 'an extra few tenths' will see Williams scoring regularly in 2022|date=26 April 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-says-an-extra-few-tenths-will-see-williams-scoring-regularly-in-2022.CFiy4pZqW7X2NsaeBmpl7.html|access-date=10 September 2022|website= Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=10 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910125028/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-says-an-extra-few-tenths-will-see-williams-scoring-regularly-in-2022.CFiy4pZqW7X2NsaeBmpl7.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon qualified eighteenth for the inaugural [[2022 Miami Grand Prix|Miami Grand Prix]] and was classified ninth, his second points score of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 May 2022 |title=Edd Straw's 2022 Miami Grand Prix F1 driver ratings |url=https://the-race.com/formula-1/edd-straws-2022-miami-grand-prix-f1-driver-ratings/ |access-date=9 May 2022 |website=The Race |language=en-GB |archive-date=9 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220509114433/https://the-race.com/formula-1/edd-straws-2022-miami-grand-prix-f1-driver-ratings/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Alonso loses Miami GP points finish after receiving 5s penalty – promoting Stroll to P10 {{!}} Formula 1® |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.alonso-loses-miami-gp-points-finish-after-receiving-5s-penalty-promoting.2hGF0jhkFxnodIrkLiDzWl.html |access-date=9 May 2022 |website=Formula 1 |language=en |archive-date=9 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220509114432/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.alonso-loses-miami-gp-points-finish-after-receiving-5s-penalty-promoting.2hGF0jhkFxnodIrkLiDzWl.html |url-status=live }}</ref> At the [[2022 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]], Albon was involved in an opening lap crash with Yuki Tsunoda and Esteban Ocon after he was hit from behind by [[Sebastian Vettel]]. He was hospitalised for precautionary checks and suffered no serious injuries.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.si.com/formula1/2022/07/03/alex-albon-medical-update-british-grand-prix-crash | title=Alex Albon Provides Medical Update Following British Grand Prix Crash | newspaper=Sports Illustrated | access-date=6 July 2022 | archive-date=6 July 2022 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706103706/https://www.si.com/formula1/2022/07/03/alex-albon-medical-update-british-grand-prix-crash | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/albon-taken-to-hospital-after-british-gp-crash/10332472/ | title=Albon taken to hospital after British GP crash | date=3 July 2022 | access-date=6 July 2022 | archive-date=6 July 2022 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706103706/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/albon-taken-to-hospital-after-british-gp-crash/10332472/ | url-status=live }}</ref>

At the [[2022 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgian Grand Prix]], Albon reached Q3 for the first time with Williams, qualifying ninth and starting sixth due to grid penalties for other drivers.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Albon hoping 'slippery' Williams can help fight for points after first Q3 appearance of 2022 in Spa|date=27 August 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-hoping-slippery-williams-can-help-fight-for-points-after-first-q3.2gN2RuH6t5k3aGvBu2lzn1.html|access-date=10 September 2022|website= Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=10 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910125044/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-hoping-slippery-williams-can-help-fight-for-points-after-first-q3.2gN2RuH6t5k3aGvBu2lzn1.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He finished the race tenth, scoring a point.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Albon says he was 'holding on for dear life' to score final point in Belgium|date=30 August 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-says-he-was-holding-on-for-dear-life-to-score-final-point-in-belgium.6KKqsElu3Px3dxB9usg47M.html|access-date=10 September 2022|website=Formula 1|language=en|archive-date=2 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220902112309/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-says-he-was-holding-on-for-dear-life-to-score-final-point-in-belgium.6KKqsElu3Px3dxB9usg47M.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon was forced to withdraw from the {{F1GP|2022|Italian}} after suffering from [[appendicitis]] and was replaced by [[Nyck de Vries]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Noble|first=Jonathan|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/de-vries-replaces-ill-albon-monza-williams/10366857/|date=10 September 2022|title=De Vries replaces ill Albon for remainder of Italian GP F1 weekend|access-date=10 September 2022|website=[[Motorsport.com]]|archive-date=10 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910103259/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/de-vries-replaces-ill-albon-monza-williams/10366857/|url-status=live}}</ref> Williams later revealed that Albon had suffered [[anaesthetic]]-related [[respiratory failure]] following his surgery but was recovering well.<ref>{{cite web |title=Albon set to return home following appendicitis surgery |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-set-to-return-home-following-appendicitis-surgery.3TEkCuPzlUx8q4Q9eDLHRu.html |website=Formula 1|access-date=13 September 2022 |archive-date=12 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220912162006/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-set-to-return-home-following-appendicitis-surgery.3TEkCuPzlUx8q4Q9eDLHRu.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He recovered in time for the {{F1 GP|2022|Singapore}}, three weeks later,<ref>{{Cite web|title='We treated it like a 9 to 5 job' – Albon details how he recovered in time for the Singapore Grand Prix|date=29 September 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.we-treated-it-like-a-9-to-5-job-albon-details-how-he-recovered-in-time-for.37QF0VAqm9yiID44caams1.html|access-date=30 September 2022|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en|archive-date=29 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220929234639/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.we-treated-it-like-a-9-to-5-job-albon-details-how-he-recovered-in-time-for.37QF0VAqm9yiID44caams1.html|url-status=live}}</ref> where he retired with damage from colliding with the barriers. He then retired on the opening lap of the {{F1 GP|2022|Japanese}} after a collision with Kevin Magnussen. An eighth-place start at the {{F1 GP|2022|United States}} failed to produce points with a thirteenth-place finish. He qualified eleventh at the {{F1 GP|2022|São Paulo}} but a puncture caused his retirement from the sprint, demoting him to the back of the grid for the race. Albon ended the season nineteenth in the World Drivers' Championship, scoring four of Williams' eight points.

==== 2023: End of Red Bull affiliation ====
[[File:FIA F1 Austria 2023 Nr. 23 (1).jpg|thumb|Albon at the [[2023 Austrian Grand Prix]]]]
Albon was retained by Williams for {{f1|2023}} on a multi-year contract, partnering [[Logan Sargeant]], who replaced Nicholas Latifi.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-williams-confirm-albon-for-2023-on-new-multi-year-contract.1rpexVv61cyqPW7h7La6LZ.html|title=Williams confirm Albon for 2023 on new multi-year contract|website=Formula 1|access-date=3 August 2022|date=3 August 2022|archive-date=3 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220803141658/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-williams-confirm-albon-for-2023-on-new-multi-year-contract.1rpexVv61cyqPW7h7La6LZ.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Williams confirm Logan Sargeant to race alongside Alex Albon for 2023|date=21 November 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-williams-confirm-logan-sargeant-to-race-alongside-alex-albon-for.4PPEcathLs1u09BIIRcNgB.html|access-date=23 November 2022|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en|archive-date=21 November 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221121130632/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-williams-confirm-logan-sargeant-to-race-alongside-alex-albon-for.4PPEcathLs1u09BIIRcNgB.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon's contract extension marked the end of his Red Bull affiliation, although he stated that he still maintained "a very close relationship" with the team; the logo of Monsoon Valley, a wine brand founded by [[Red Bull GmbH|Red Bull]] co-owner [[Chalerm Yoovidhya]], features on his race helmet.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://the-race.com/formula-1/albon-independent-from-red-bull-under-new-williams-deal/|title=Albon independent from Red Bull under new Williams F1 deal|website=the-race.com|first1=Scott|last1=Mitchell-Malm|first2=Valentin|last2=Khorounzhiy|date=25 August 2022|accessdate=16 March 2023|archive-date=1 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220901100136/https://the-race.com/formula-1/albon-independent-from-red-bull-under-new-williams-deal/|url-status=live}}</ref> He qualified fifteenth at the season-opening {{F1 GP|2023|Bahrain}}, failing to set a time in Q2 due to front wing damage.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/b6932f28-c469-4ecd-af02-e9e8e176544f/2023-bahrain-grand-prix-qualifying|title=2023 Bahrain Grand Prix Qualifying|website=Williams Racing|date=4 March 2023|accessdate=16 March 2023|archive-date=14 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230414135950/https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/b6932f28-c469-4ecd-af02-e9e8e176544f/2023-bahrain-grand-prix-qualifying|url-status=live}}</ref> He recovered in the race to score a point with tenth place. He retired from the {{F1 GP|2023|Saudi Arabian}} with a brake failure. He qualified eighth at the {{F1 GP|2023|Australian}} and ran as high as sixth in the opening laps, but crashed heavily on lap six. At the {{F1 GP|2023|Azerbaijan}}, he qualified and finished the sprint race in the top ten, but failed to score points after finishing the main race twelfth.
[[File:Alexander Albon - 2023 Italian Grand Prix.jpg|thumb|left|Albon at the [[2023 Italian Grand Prix]]]]
Albon started ninth at the {{F1 GP|2023|Canadian}}, executed a successful one-stop strategy and held behind the faster cars of Sergio Pérez and George Russell for much of the race to finish seventh, his best result thus far for Williams. He received praise from Red Bull team principal Christian Horner for his performance.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.co.uk/f1/story/_/id/37875718/alex-albon-gets-driver-day-defensive-masterclass-canada|title=Alex Albon gets Driver of the Day with defensive masterclass|website=espn.co.uk|date=19 June 2023|accessdate=10 July 2023|archive-date=10 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230710203711/https://www.espn.co.uk/f1/story/_/id/37875718/alex-albon-gets-driver-day-defensive-masterclass-canada|url-status=live}}</ref> Another Q3 appearance came at the {{F1 GP|2023|Austrian}} followed by an eleventh-place finish. He qualified in the top ten for the third consecutive race at the {{F1 GP|2023|British}} and finished ahead of both [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]]s in eighth place. He matched his highest career qualifying position at the {{F1 GP|2023|Dutch}}, starting fourth.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motorsportstats.com/driver/alexander-albon/stats/series/formula-one/bestGridPosition|title=Alexander Albon Advanced stats|website=motorsportstats.com|access-date=26 August 2023|archive-date=6 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230906163344/https://www.motorsportstats.com/driver/alexander-albon/stats/series/formula-one/bestGridPosition|url-status=live}}</ref> Despite staying on [[Racing slicks|slick tyres]] during a rain shower in the early laps and dropping to fifteenth place, he recovered to finish eighth.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.racefans.net/2023/08/28/williams-on-par-with-ferrari-and-aston-martin-at-zandvoort-albon/|title=Williams "on par with Ferrari and Aston Martin" at Zandvoort – Albon|website=racefans.net|first1=Ida|last1=Wood|first2=Claire|last2=Cottingham|date=28 August 2023|accessdate=28 August 2023|archive-date=28 August 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230828223548/https://www.racefans.net/2023/08/28/williams-on-par-with-ferrari-and-aston-martin-at-zandvoort-albon/|url-status=live}}</ref> He scored points again at the {{F1 GP|2023|Italian}} where he qualified sixth and defended against [[Lando Norris]] to finish seventh.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.im-sure-lando-didnt-enjoy-it-albon-reflects-on-more-hard-earned-points-after.5zZY1I3uG0EBkRNe586Hmb.html|title='I'm sure Lando didn't enjoy it!' – Albon reflects on more hard-earned points after pipping Norris to P7 at Monza|website=Formula 1|date=4 September 2023|accessdate=5 September 2023|archive-date=5 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230905210034/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.im-sure-lando-didnt-enjoy-it-albon-reflects-on-more-hard-earned-points-after.5zZY1I3uG0EBkRNe586Hmb.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:Alex Albon.jpg|thumb|Albon speaking to the media at the launch event for the [[Williams FW46]] at the Puma Flagship Store in [[New York City]]]]
Albon started seventeenth for the {{F1 GP|2023|Qatar}} sprint and gained ten places to score points in seventh place. He received two penalties for track limits infringements and failed to score in the main race. More points came at the [[2023 United States Grand Prix|United States]] and [[2023 Mexico City Grand Prix|Mexico City]] Grands Prix; he finished ninth in both races having started outside the top ten. He was then eliminated in a first-corner collision with Kevin Magnussen at the {{F1 GP|2023|São Paulo}}, having started thirteenth. Albon and teammate Sargeant started fifth and sixth respectively for the {{F1 GP|2023|Las Vegas}}, but both failed to score in the race (with Albon finishing 12th), due to the timing of safety car going against them.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.racefans.net/2023/11/19/albons-las-vegas-grand-prix-an-endless-cycle-of-pain/ | title=Albon's Las Vegas Grand Prix "an endless cycle of pain" | date=19 November 2023 }}</ref> Albon ended the season thirteenth in the Drivers' Championship. He scored 27 points to Sargeant's one point, securing Williams seventh place in the Constructors' Championship.

==== 2024: Setbacks at Williams ====
[[File:Alex Albon 2024 Chinese GP.jpg|thumb|left|Albon at the [[2024 Chinese Grand Prix]]]]
Albon continued at Williams alongside Sargeant for {{F1|2024}}. He qualified and finished outside the top ten at the season-opening {{F1 GP|2024|Bahrain}}, with the team stating that both drivers were dealing with engine overheating issues during the race.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/a0f3866e-deed-4210-a73c-729a9475726f/report-bahrain-2024-race|title=REPORT: Issues hamper progress at season opener|website=Williams Racing|date=2 March 2024|accessdate=1 April 2024}}</ref> He and Kevin Magnussen collided at the {{F1 GP|2024|Saudi Arabian}} and Albon went on to finish eleventh.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-frustrated-not-to-score-points-after-tough-race-in-saudi-arabia-amid.5ZbpANDy4Z4JTWaaqeTdtY|title=Albon frustrated not to score points after 'tough' race in Saudi Arabia amid clash with Magnussen|website=Formula 1|date=14 March 2024|accessdate=1 April 2024}}</ref>

Albon crashed in the first practice session at the {{F1 GP|2024|Australian}} and Williams were unable to repair his car, nor did they have a spare chassis. Team principal [[James Vowles]] described the situation as "a reflection of how behind we were in the winter period".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/bc29d1d1-8148-41e5-8c6e-59d452682be6/australian-grand-prix-update|title=Australian Grand Prix Update|website=Williams Racing|date=22 March 2024|accessdate=1 April 2024}}</ref> The team decided to withdraw Sargeant from the event and allow Albon to use the one remaining car. He finished the race eleventh. He qualified fourteenth for the {{F1 GP|2024|Japanese}} but was eliminated in a first-lap crash with Daniel Ricciardo.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.crash.net/f1/news/1046572/1/alex-albon-details-williams-woe-after-daniel-ricciardo-crash-f1-japanese-gp|title=Alex Albon details Williams woe after Daniel Ricciardo crash at F1 Japanese GP|website=crash.net|first=James|last=Dielhenn|date=7 April 2024|accessdate=8 April 2024}}</ref> On 15 May, Williams confirmed that Albon has signed a multi-year extension to stay with the team.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/85039339-fe5d-4734-ad05-eab805ef651a/alex-albon-here-to-stay|title=Alex Albon commits future to Williams Racing|date=15 May 2024|access-date=15 May 2024|work=[[Williams Grand Prix Engineering|Williams Racing]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240515093456/https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/85039339-fe5d-4734-ad05-eab805ef651a/alex-albon-here-to-stay|archive-date=15 May 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

Albon did not score points in the next three races which included him retiring in [[2024 Emilia Romagna Grand Prix|Imola]] due to issues putting the tyre on at his pit stop taking him out of the points battle.<ref name="race report">{{cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/verstappen-holds-off-thrilling-late-charge-from-norris-to-win-emilia-romagna.2CgBWm3GodjE3QIYIMWL0x |title=Verstappen holds off thrilling late charge from Norris to win Emilia-Romagna Grand Prix |date=20 May 2024 |website=Formula 1}}</ref> Looking to bounce back Albon qualified ninth at the [[2024 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco Grand Prix]]. He then kept hold of his position to finish in 9th place which marked his and Williams first points of the season.<ref>{{cite news |date=26 May 2024 |title=Leclerc clinches long-awaited home win in Monaco ahead of Piastri and Sainz after early drama |website=Formula 1|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/leclerc-clinches-long-awaited-home-win-in-monaco-ahead-of-piastri-and-sainz.4EmeQeb2P27aS8yGIVMOEO|access-date=26 May 2024}}</ref> Albon then qualified 10th for the [[2024 Canadian Grand Prix|Canadian Grand Prix]]<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2024/races/1237/canada/qualifying.html |title=Formula 1 AWS Grand Prix du Canada 2024 - Qualifying |website=Formula 1 |access-date=15 July 2024}}</ref> but retired from the race whilst running in the points on lap 52 after Carlos Sainz spun into him sending him into the wall.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/passenger-albon-left-frustrated-by-sainz-crash-on-day-points-were-possible.4pRPhr0hqtrrmkiZd7oyW4 |title='Passenger' Albon left frustrated by Sainz crash on day points were possible, as Sargeant rues 'too many mistakes' |date=10 June 2024 |website=Formula 1 |access-date=15 July 2024}}</ref>

After finishing out of the points in [[2024 Spanish Grand Prix|Spain]] and [[2024 Austrian Grand Prix|Austria]], Albon then earned his second points finish of the season by finishing ninth at the [[2024 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]]<ref>{{cite news |date=7 July 2024 |title=Hamilton beats Verstappen to first win since 2021 with record-breaking 9th British Grand Prix victory |website=Formula 1|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/hamilton-beats-verstappen-to-first-win-since-2021-with-record-breaking-9th.3teU9bznaWJlC2TGAYh0Vl}}</ref> This meant at the halfway point of the season Albon was 17th in the drivers' championship with four points. After the [[2024 Dutch Grand Prix|Dutch Grand Prix]], [[Franco Colapinto]] became Albon's new teammate from the [[2024 Italian Grand Prix|Italian Grand Prix]] onwards. At Monza, Albon scored Williams' third points finish of the season taking another 9th place. During the {{F1GP|2024|Azerbaijan}}, Albon benefitted from a late crash between [[Sergio Pérez]] and [[Carlos Sainz Jr.]], which saw Albon finish seventh, allowing Williams to overtake Alpine in the [[World Constructors' Championship]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=REPORT: Double points finish in Baku |url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/f1d84f28-9b9b-4307-ad8a-fad29d34bd01/report-2024-azerbaijan-grand-prix |date=15 September 2024 |access-date=29 August 2025 |website=Atlassian Williams Racing }}</ref>

Albon did not start the {{F1GP|2024|São Paulo}} after crashing heavily in the third qualifying session; qualifying had been rescheduled to Sunday morning, leaving Williams unable to repair his chassis in time for the race two hours later.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Albon to miss Sao Paulo GP after heavy qualifying crash |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/breaking-williams-confirm-albon-to-miss-sao-paulo-grand-prix-after-heavy.7qoBKIwX36dJHwSsvtRFsJ |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=Formula 1 |language=en}}</ref> A cooling issue forced Albon to retire midway through the {{F1GP|2024|Las Vegas}}, while he finished 15th and 11th in the {{F1GP|2024|Qatar}} and {{F1GP|2024|Abu Dhabi}}, respectively.<ref>{{cite web |title=2024 Driver Standings: Alexander Albon |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results/2024/drivers/ALEALB01/alexander-albon |website=Formula 1 |access-date=12 March 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Harrington |first1=Alex |title=Williams F1 Team Reveals Why Alex Albon Retired from Las Vegas Grand Prix |url=https://www.si.com/fannation/racing/f1briefings/news/williams-f1-team-reveals-why-alex-albon-retired-from-las-vegas-grand-prix-01jdeqn8sn9h |access-date=12 March 2025 |agency=F1 on SI |date=24 November 2024 |archive-date=26 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241126230936/https://www.si.com/fannation/racing/f1briefings/news/williams-f1-team-reveals-why-alex-albon-retired-from-las-vegas-grand-prix-01jdeqn8sn9h |url-status=dead }}</ref> Albon finished the season 16th in the Drivers' Championship with 12 points.<ref>{{cite web |title=2024 Driver Standings |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results/2024/drivers |website=Formula 1 |access-date=12 March 2025}}</ref>

==== 2025: Partnership with Sainz ====
[[File:2025 Japan GP - Williams - Alex Albon - FP2.jpg|thumb|right|Albon (pictured at the {{F1GP|2025|Japanese}}) is partnered by [[Carlos Sainz Jr.]] from {{F1|2025}} onwards|alt=Albon driving the Williams FW47 at the 2025 Japanese Grand Prix]]
Albon partnered [[Carlos Sainz Jr.]] starting from {{F1|2025}}.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-shares-clear-2025-goal-for-williams-after-teams-eye-catching-display.5SAnRW9FTYIin02ZvkyYQO |title=Albon shares clear 2025 goal for Williams after team's eye-catching display at pre-season testing |date=13 March 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]] |publisher=[[Formula One Group]] |access-date=26 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250315203006/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-shares-clear-2025-goal-for-williams-after-teams-eye-catching-display.5SAnRW9FTYIin02ZvkyYQO |archive-date=15 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He qualified sixth and finished fifth at the rain-affected {{F1GP|2025|Australian}}.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/f1/alex-albon-williams-f1-australian-gp-carlos-sainz-b2715983.html |title=Alex Albon bright-eyed for Williams future after terrific Australian GP: 'It will be an exciting year' |last=Hicks |first=Olivia |date=16 March 2025 |work=[[The Independent]] |access-date=26 March 2025 |issn=1741-9743 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250317195033/https://www.independent.co.uk/f1/alex-albon-williams-f1-australian-gp-carlos-sainz-b2715983.html |archive-date=17 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He then finished seventh at the {{F1GP|2025|Chinese}} after disqualifications for [[Charles Leclerc]] and [[Lewis Hamilton]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/leclerc-hamilton-and-gasly-disqualified-from-chinese-gp/10706422/ |title=Leclerc, Hamilton and Gasly disqualified from Chinese GP |last=Cleeren |first=Filip |date=23 March 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=23 March 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250323122436/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/leclerc-hamilton-and-gasly-disqualified-from-chinese-gp/10706422/ |archive-date=23 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He achieved a further points finish with ninth in [[2025 Japanese Grand Prix|Japan]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/alex-albon-addresses-heated-team-radio-messages-with-williams-in-suzuka/10710488/ |title=Alex Albon addresses heated team radio messages with engineer at Japan GP |last=Mee |first=Lydia |date=6 April 2025 |website=[[Motorsport.com]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=6 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250406180102/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/alex-albon-addresses-heated-team-radio-messages-with-williams-in-suzuka/10710488/ |archive-date=6 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> before claiming twelfth in [[2025 Bahrain Grand Prix|Bahrain]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-rues-missed-opportunity-as-he-fails-to-score-for-the-first-time-this.1TFmi0sRVKacBomaWwUgI9 |title=Frustrated Albon rues 'missed opportunity' as he fails to score for the first time in 2025 |date=14 April 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]] |publisher=[[Formula One Group]] |access-date=15 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250415161849/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-rues-missed-opportunity-as-he-fails-to-score-for-the-first-time-this.1TFmi0sRVKacBomaWwUgI9 |archive-date=15 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He returned to ninth at the {{F1GP|2025|Saudi Arabian}}.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-and-sainz-praise-teamwork-as-saudi-arabia-double-points-finish-puts.6ocVwwpbmYt01zJoWPAlwx |title=Albon and Sainz praise teamwork as Saudi Arabia double points finish puts Williams fifth in standings |date=21 April 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]] |publisher=[[Formula One Group]] |access-date=21 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250421215406/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-and-sainz-praise-teamwork-as-saudi-arabia-double-points-finish-puts.6ocVwwpbmYt01zJoWPAlwx |archive-date=21 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Albon finished fifth again in both [[2025 Miami Grand Prix|Miami]] and [[2025 Emilia Romagna Grand Prix|Emilia-Romagna]], scoring ten points each.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-delighted-with-fifth-in-miami-as-sainz-admits-frustrations-over.3p2MYXjGGawKwmJqVFPCND|title=Albon delighted with fifth in Miami as Sainz admits frustrations over inability to score 'bigger' points for Williams|website=[[Formula 1]]|date=5 May 2025|access-date=24 September 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-left-with-mixed-feelings-over-p5-finish-in-imola-after-licking-my-lips.20gLcDczAq2QN3ZQyshdlL|title=Albon left with mixed feelings over P5 finish in Imola after 'licking my lips' at potential Williams podium|website=[[Formula 1]]|date=18 May 2025|access-date=24 September 2025}}</ref> After [[did not finish|not finishing]] in three straight races–[[2025 Spanish Grand Prix|Spain]], [[2025 Canadian Grand Prix|Canada]], and [[2025 Austrian Grand Prix|Austria]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/sainz-calls-for-williams-to-regroup-as-albon-admits-team-need-to-stop-issues.1akCKGthjimz07wYI7VRP4|title=Sainz calls for Williams to 'regroup' as Albon admits team 'need to stop' issues after double DNF in Austria|date=29 June 2025|access-date=24 September 2025|website=[[Formula 1]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-predicts-a-dogfight-for-midfield-supremacy-as-he-vows-to-fight-like.5G4IIvtjLT0RuEI02cznmK|title=Albon predicts a 'dogfight' for midfield supremacy as he vows to 'fight like hell' to keep P5 for Williams|date=5 August 2025|access-date=24 September 2025|website=[[Formula 1]]}}</ref>–he finished eighth in [[2025 British Grand Prix|Great Britain]] and sixth in [[2025 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgium]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Albon 'very happy' to return to points at Silverstone as Sainz rues 'really frustrating' run of bad luck|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-very-happy-to-return-to-points-at-silverstone-as-sainz-rues-really.3u2zSGLPs5YMQTrciVolkg|website=[[Formula 1]]|access-date=24 September 2025|date=9 July 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Albon 'happy' with P6 in Belgium after holding off Hamilton but Sainz not 'clicking' with FW47 |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-happy-with-p6-in-belgium-after-holding-off-hamilton-but-sainz-not.6z1kNK5iVa3dsHwT8y6oLx |date=28 July 2025 |access-date=24 September 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]]}}</ref> Later on, he finished fifth again in the [[2025 Dutch Grand Prix|Netherlands]] and seventh at [[2025 Italian Grand Prix|Monza]], earning ten and six points respectively.<ref>{{Cite web |title='Everyone just seemed to crash' – Albon thrilled with P5 in Dutch Grand Prix after 'a little bit of luck' |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/everyone-just-seemed-to-crash-albon-thrilled-with-p5-in-dutch-grand-prix.ZFS6Jovw01NpSzrPKyubk |date=2 September 2025 |access-date=24 September 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Alex Albon hails 'one of the best Sundays of the year' in Italy while Sainz rules another 'difficult day' |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-hails-one-of-the-best-sundays-of-the-year-in-italy-while-sainz-rues.6tuVoVUr3fOhpiu8MApuIv |date=8 September 2025 |access-date=24 September 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]]}}</ref> His final points of the year came in the [[2025 United States Grand Prix|COTA sprint race]], in which he finished sixth.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cleeren |first=Filip |date=2026-06-23 |title=F1 US GP: Verstappen wins sprint race, Norris and Piastri taken out in start crash |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/f1-us-gp-verstappen-wins-sprint-race-norris-and-piastri-taken-out-in-start-crash/10768885/ |access-date=2026-06-24 |website=Autosport |language=en}}</ref> Albon finished the season in 8th place in the Drivers' Championship with 73 points, ahead of his teammate.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Formula 1 2025 results and standings for top drivers and teams |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/standings/2025/ |access-date=2026-06-24 |website=Motorsport.com |language=en}}</ref>

==== 2026: New regulations ====
Albon remained at [[Williams Racing|Williams]] alongside [[Carlos Sainz Jr.|Carlos Sainz Jr]] for the [[2026 Formula One World Championship#Regulation changes|new power unit and chassis regulations]] in [[2026 Formula One World Championship|2026]]. He opened the season with a twelfth place finish at the [[2026 Australian Grand Prix|Australian Grand Prix]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Perin |first=Chiara |date=2026-03-09 |title=Albon discusses Williams struggles at 2026 F1 Australian GP |url=https://www.pitdebrief.com/post/albon-discusses-williams-struggles-at-2026-f1-australian-gp/ |access-date=2026-07-16 |website=Pit Debrief |language=en-GB}}</ref> before failing to start at the [[2026 Chinese Grand Prix|Chinese Grand Prix]] because of a hydraulics issue.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jogia |first=Saajan |date=2026-03-15 |title=Explained: What Caused the Chinese Grand Prix DNSs for McLaren, Audi and Williams? |url=https://www.motorbiscuit.com/explained-what-caused-the-chinese-grand-prix-dnss-for-mclaren-audi-and-williams/ |access-date=2026-07-16 |website=MotorBiscuit |language=en-US}}</ref> He scored his first point of the season with a tenth place finish at the [[2026 Miami Grand Prix|Miami Grand Prix]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-05-11 |title=Alex Albon makes Williams F1 warning after double Miami points |url=https://www.crash.net/f1/news/1094898/1/alex-albon-makes-williams-f1-warning-after-double-miami-points |access-date=2026-07-16 |website=Crash.net |language=en|last=Hall|first=Sam|last2=Cooper|first2=Adam}}</ref> but did not finish the following race in [[2026 Canadian Grand Prix|Canada]] after a collision with [[McLaren]] driver [[Oscar Piastri]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Albon reflects on Piastri collision that led to Canada DNF |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/not-ideal-albon-reflects-on-unfortunate-collision-with-piastri-that-put-him-out-of-canadian-grand-prix.7IscvoqeFsmwvaged75jak |access-date=2026-07-16 |website=Formula 1 |language=en|date=2026-05-25}}</ref> Albon scored four more points with an eighth place finish at the [[2026 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-06-06 |title=F1 Results - 2026 Monaco Grand Prix |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/2026/monaco-grand-prix/results |access-date=2026-07-16 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref> At [[2026 Barcelona-Catalunya Grand Prix|Barcelona]], Albon finished the race but was not classified as he did not complete 90% of the race distance because of a mechanical issue.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lopes |first=Nicolas |date=2026-06-21 |title=Albon explains why his Barcelona-Catalunya F1 GP turned into a "testing session" |url=https://www.pitdebrief.com/post/albon-explains-why-barcelona-catalunya-gp-turned-into-testing-session/ |access-date=2026-07-16 |website=Pit Debrief |language=en-GB}}</ref> He retired from the race at [[2026 British Grand Prix|Silverstone]] after a collision with [[Haas F1 Team|Haas]] driver [[Oliver Bearman|Ollie Bearman]] on the first lap.<ref>{{Cite web |title=First lap crash at Silverstone ‘was on me’ – Albon |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/albon-takes-blame-for-first-lap-silverstone-crash-after-tough-williams-weekend.2tVYDdIl8fYtR0qetrgTYi |access-date=2026-07-16 |website=Formula 1 |language=en|date=2026-07-08}}</ref> He finished fifteenth in [[2026 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgium]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Belgium 2026 - Result • STATS F1 |url=https://www.statsf1.com/en/2026/belgique/classement.aspx |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=www.statsf1.com}}</ref> and seventeenth in [[2026 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungary]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hungary 2026 - Result • STATS F1 |url=https://www.statsf1.com/en/2026/hongrie/classement.aspx |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=www.statsf1.com}}</ref>

== Other racing ==
=== Formula E (2017) ===
[[File:AlexAlbonMarrakech2017.jpg|thumb|200px|Albon at Formula E testing in [[Marrakesh]], December 2017]]
Albon was signed by [[DAMS|Nissan e.dams]] alongside [[Sébastien Buemi]] as one of its drivers for the [[2018–19 Formula E season]], but he was released before the start of the season to instead drive in the [[2019 Formula One World Championship|2019 Formula One season]] for [[Scuderia Toro Rosso|Toro Rosso]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.motorsport.com/us/formula-e/news/nissan-signs-albon-as-buemis-formula-e-teammate/3180107/|title=Nissan signs Albon as Buemi's Formula E teammate|work=Motorsport.com|access-date=24 September 2018|language=en|archive-date=24 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180924224804/https://www.motorsport.com/us/formula-e/news/nissan-signs-albon-as-buemis-formula-e-teammate/3180107/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":0" />

Albon's place was taken by former F2 colleague [[Oliver Rowland]], who previously competed in the [[2015 Punta del Este ePrix]] as an injury replacement for [[Mahindra Racing]]'s [[Nick Heidfeld]].

=== DTM (2021) ===
[[File:AlexAlbonHockenheimDTM.jpg|thumb|left|Albon at the [[Hockenheimring]] in 2021]]
Albon participated in 14 out of 16 races of the [[2021 Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters]], with [[Formula E]] driver [[Nick Cassidy]] taking his place for the final two races at the [[Norisring]]. He was driving for the Italian outfit [[AF Corse]] alongside [[FIA Formula 2 Championship|Formula 2]] driver [[Liam Lawson]], with financial backing from [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] and [[AlphaTauri (fashion brand)|AlphaTauri]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Thukral|first=Rachit|date=4 January 2021|title=Albon to contest DTM in 2021 with Red Bull backing alongside Lawson|url=https://www.autosport.com/dtm/news/154439/albon-to-contest-dtm-in-2021-with-red-bull-backing|access-date=4 January 2021|website=Autosport.com|language=en|archive-date=4 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210104141356/https://www.autosport.com/dtm/news/154439/albon-to-contest-dtm-in-2021-with-red-bull-backing|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 22 August 2021, Albon won his maiden DTM race at the [[Nürburgring]], becoming the first Thai driver to win a DTM race.<ref>{{Cite web|date=19 June 2021|title=AlphaTauri Driver Alex Albon Scores Maiden DTM Win – Kelvin Van Der Linde Victim Of The Hot Race And Still Half-Time Champion|url=https://www.dtm.com/en/news/alphatauri-driver-alex-albon-scores-maiden-dtm-win|website=DTM.com|language=en-GB|access-date=3 September 2021|archive-date=23 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210823004556/https://www.dtm.com/en/news/alphatauri-driver-alex-albon-scores-maiden-dtm-win|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Albon holds [[Dual national|dual]] British and Thai nationality, and races under the Thai flag for [[sponsorship]] reasons.<ref name="independent.co.uk">{{Cite web|date=23 October 2019|title=Alex Albon hoping to earn new Red Bull deal and move out of parents' house|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/motor-racing/formula1/f1-news-alex-albon-red-bull-deal-christian-horner-george-russell-lewis-hamilton-a9167761.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191024164857/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/motor-racing/formula1/f1-news-alex-albon-red-bull-deal-christian-horner-george-russell-lewis-hamilton-a9167761.html |archive-date=24 October 2019 |url-access=limited |url-status=live|access-date=30 July 2020|website=The Independent|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://uk.sports.yahoo.com/news/exclusive-alex-albon-reflects-on-rookie-formula-1-season-154704486.html |date=4 December 2019 |last=Parkes |first=Ian |title=Exclusive: Why British-born Alex Albon is a Thai racing hero |website=Yahoo! Sport |access-date=7 June 2025}}</ref> He is a practicing [[Buddhism|Buddhist]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Albon reveals he is a 'practising Buddhist'|url=https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/fb_361975.html|access-date=30 July 2020|website=Sports Mole|date=14 June 2019 |language=en-GB|archive-date=29 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029085935/https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/fb_361975.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Albon and his family own a number of pets, consisting of at least twelve cats, a dog and two horses.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Confused by Alex's British GP lid? Here's everything you need to know about Albon Pets!|url=https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/8a46e499-54ff-4b41-a80f-52a5e43aac8a/everything-you-need-to-know-about-albon-pets|date=1 July 2022|access-date=6 October 2022|website=Williams Racing|language=en-GB|archive-date=5 October 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221005105334/https://www.williamsf1.com/posts/8a46e499-54ff-4b41-a80f-52a5e43aac8a/everything-you-need-to-know-about-albon-pets|url-status=live}}</ref> He has been publicly dating Chinese [[LPGA]] golfer [[Muni He|Lily He]] since 2019 after mutually developing interest in each other's sport.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 April 2022 |title=Formula 1's Alex Albon on his LPGA girlfriend Muni He, Netflix drama and the golf craze that's hit elite race-car drivers |url=https://golfweek.usatoday.com/2022/04/22/formula-1-alex-albon-lpga-muni-he-netflix-drive-survive/ |access-date=22 January 2023 |website=Golfweek |language=en-US |archive-date=22 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230122120112/https://golfweek.usatoday.com/2022/04/22/formula-1-alex-albon-lpga-muni-he-netflix-drive-survive/ |url-status=live }}</ref>  The couple announced their engagement in January 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Golding |first1=Nick |title=Alex Albon takes to social media to announce engagement |url=https://racingnews365.com/alex-albon-takes-to-social-media-to-announce-engagement |date=16 January 2026 |access-date=16 January 2026 |work=RacingNews365 |language=en-UK}}</ref>

In interviews, Albon has spoken publicly about the mental pressure during his Formula 1 career. Whilst he was at [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]], he stated he was "destroyed mentally" from the pressures and criticism, and later he turned to a psychologist to improve his mental well-being and performance.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Albon opens up about his Red Bull struggles |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/every-mistake-gets-criticised-albon-opens-up-on-red-bull-struggles-and-how.FOd2iLkoK5mdWocUhCoiQ |access-date=16 November 2025 |website=www.formula1.com |language=en}}</ref>

Albon has worked with performance coach and physiotherapist, Patrick Harding, who has supported him throughout his Formula 1 career, including at both Red Bull and [[Williams Racing|Williams]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=ALL IN OR GET OUT: Training an F1 Driver - Patrick Harding {{!}} FanAmp Insider |url=https://www.fanamp.com/articles/fanamp-insider-patrick-harding-interview-2025 |access-date=2026-02-23 |website=www.fanamp.com |language=en}}</ref>

Albon is childhood friends with fellow Formula 1 driver [[George Russell (racing driver)|George Russell]], racing together since junior karting days. <ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qDs3mFD-t3M |title=George Russell & Alex Albon Take On The Compliment Challenge! |date=2024-09-28 |last=FORMULA 1 |access-date=2026-06-19 |via=YouTube}}</ref>

